Mor-Mordehai Tziada, the eldest son of Yael and Eliasaf, was born on 10 Elul 5741 (9 September 1981) in Haifa. He was the brother of Hamutal, Yahalom, Oriya, Avishai and Amitai. "Mor" was the name everyone called him by, but his name was Mordehai, after his uncle, who was killed in a road accident shortly before Mor was born. Mor was both the eldest son and the eldest grandson, beloved by all the members of the extended family. His aunts wrote: "The 'first grandchild' brought great joy to our home and added to all our names more titles: grandfather and grandmother, uncle, aunt and parents. We wrapped him in a great deal of love, and every word he uttered filled us with pride. We learned many children's songs from him, and we went out dancing with him. Even when we started families of our own, the songs we had learned together accompanied us."

From both grandfathers he inherited a love of Torah and the commandments. His mother Yael's father was a well-known figure in Tirat HaCarmel, a teacher and educator. From his grandmothers he received modesty and humility, and from his parents he learned respect and love for others, honoring father and mother, and humanity. He was raised on love of the Land of Israel and the Torah of Israel. His mother was a teacher in the local school system, and his father was a hospital rabbi at the Mental Health Center in Tirat HaCarmel. Mor grew up in Tirat HaCarmel and attended the kindergarten of the teacher Parhia Gabai, who wrote after his death: "Mor, son of Eliasaf and Yael, a unique family, excelling in values and love of people, an example to the people of Israel. The first child of a family of educators.

// Mor, curly-haired and beautiful-eyed, with a golden smile on his lips and light in his eyes, pleasant in his manner, outstanding in his broad knowledge and rich language, and he was only three years old.

// A leader in his virtues, modest in character, an example to his friends, and he was only three years old.

// On Sunday mornings at the morning circle he displayed his wide knowledge of the weekly Torah portion, and with great enthusiasm and charm he made sure to share with us the verses special to that portion, and he was only three years old.

// With his talent and broad knowledge he raised the level of our conversations and stories. A happy and cheerful child, he loved to help everyone, like a mature and responsible child, and he was only three years old.

// We loved him and waited for him. A severe tragedy struck us; this precious lad was taken from us, and he was only twenty-one."

Mor studied at the Ephraim Tzemach elementary school and continued to middle school and high school at the Yavneh yeshiva in Haifa. He was a diligent student, liked by his friends. His brother Avishai recalled: "I remember that talking to him was a pleasure. He knew how to really listen, to listen with his eyes and not in the air, to truly listen, even to small children. I would seize opportunities on the way from home to the synagogue and back just to talk with him, about anything at all, just so he would listen, so he would be with me... If someone heard a joke at home, they would run to tell Mor, just to see him laugh, glowing with joy. He would make everyone around him happy, and just seeing him laugh would make them laugh. Sometimes we would ask: 'Wait for me, I want to be there when you tell him,' and we'd argue over who would tell the joke. He had a strong power of speech. A word from him was never mumbling, or a string of words unconnected to what he was saying. It was always alive... When Mor said a word, it was a word, and we would often say, 'Mor said.'"

Mor joined the Bnei Akiva movement in its Tirat HaCarmel branch, and in time became a beloved and admired madrich (youth leader) to his members. He loved his members very much and instilled in them love of Torah, love of people and love of the Land of Israel, the values he had grown up with and lived himself. Much of his free time was devoted to activities in Bnei Akiva, and together with a renewed leadership team the branch grew stronger and changed its character, becoming a center of social activity for religious Zionist youth in Tirat HaCarmel. At the end of his high school studies he moved to the Hesder yeshiva "Midbara K'Eden" in Mitzpe Ramon, where he was able to fulfill his great love of Torah. Mor enjoyed every moment at the yeshiva: the study, the friends and the atmosphere. His great joy in life, his modesty and simplicity won everyone over. When he was about to be drafted into the IDF, he decided to enlist in the Golani Brigade and to part from his friends at the hesder yeshiva; that is, to fulfill his duty in full-time regular service for the full period of three years, without the concessions granted to yeshiva students. He enlisted on 12 March 2001.

Mor fought in Operation "Defensive Shield" in the towns of Tulkarm, Jenin, Hebron and Nablus. He took part in the battle in which the company commander Avihu Yaakov fell and the battalion commander Ofek Buchris was seriously wounded. Despite the danger, Mor saw his service as a mission. Towards the end of his training he was sent to a medic course, and after his unit went to the front he began serving in the important role of company medic in the Golani 51st Battalion. He was a good and beloved soldier in his company, with a strong character and high abilities. His comrades in the unit tell that he was a good, caring, serious and responsible friend. Even in his role as medic, his special qualities – modesty, calm and a unique sense of humor – brought smiles and helped every sick and wounded person. He always volunteered to carry out his tasks without exceptions and with full dedication, taking care never to neglect Torah study and even setting fixed times for it. The battalion moved to Har Dov to guard the northern border, and Mor served as medic and driver of an armored personnel carrier (an M113). Although he was a veteran soldier, he did not give up on any activity and worked with full energy. Even when his father fell ill, Mor returned to his operational duties out of responsibility and moral commitment, and from a distance he looked after his father's welfare. Mor was very attached to his family and cared for them deeply, especially for his sick father. He was due to be released on a short leave because of his father's hospitalization, but since he did not want to abandon his comrades, he chose to stay and take part in the operational activity on the Saturday from which he did not return.

On the eve of Passover the battalion was sent on operational deployment in Jenin, the city where many battalion soldiers had fallen in the past. Mor was excited to take on the responsibility of company medic in an intensive operational deployment, and he prepared himself well for it. But he did not have time to serve long in this role. On 13 Sivan 5763 (13 June 2003) Mor set out on his last operational mission – the capture of a wanted man. On the way out of the city, at the end of the operation, a few minutes before the start of the Sabbath, a terrorist fired a burst of gunfire from within a crowd at the military vehicle in which Mor was riding. A single bullet pierced the armor plating of the jeep from behind and struck Mor in the neck, killing him. Mor Mordehai Tziada fell in operational activity in Jenin. He was twenty-two years old. He was laid to rest in the military cemetery in Haifa. He left behind his parents, two sisters and three brothers. After his death he was promoted to Staff Sergeant First Class. The family received the news of their son's death on Friday evening, at the Sabbath table. The battalion commander eulogized Mor and noted his heroism and determination in every mission. His comrades and family will always remember him smiling, telling jokes, and doing everything with the simplicity and humility that characterized him.

The song "UVehaalotah et HaNerot" by Sarah Cohen, Mor's aunt, set to music by Idan Sharon, was performed by the group "Kol HaLev" and recorded. The song tells of Mor's death on the Sabbath eve in the portion of "Behaalotecha," at the time of lighting the Sabbath candles, and it was released as a single:

"And on the Sabbath eve, as she lit the candles, / she asked with tears of the One enthroned on high: / Please, watch over my children and send complete healing to my husband, / and my soldier son Mor, please, O God, strengthen and protect him. / And she did not know, and her prayer rose up to heaven, / and the angels there wept.

// And on the Sabbath eve, as she lit the candles, / his soul already stood before the throne of glory, / and she asked that his mother endure the decree and that his father recover speedily. / And the ministering angels wept as they descended to escort the Sabbath, / and she did not know...

// And on another Sabbath eve she will light the candles again / and pray quietly with tears, / and who knows her prayer? / And up there, together with her, / her soldier son will ask for what she does not ask, / for his mother whom he loved so much: strength of body and soul."

A family friend, Uziel Tziada, wrote: "Mor Mordehai Tziada, may God avenge his blood, / we will remember you forever, / for you were unique and special, / a friend of people and of God. / Young, you departed for the sanctification of God's Name; / for us here there is no comfort. / A unique and true Jew, / who walked according to the divine message, / who combined the yoke of Torah and the yoke of the kingdom, / and upright living full of quality. / Alas for the lost, who are not forgotten; / happy is he who knew how to prepare for the journey. / His place is beneath the Throne of Glory, / among angels full of majesty, / who embrace with love his good deeds, / for in his death he commanded us life. / Rest in peace, pure soul, / until body and soul are reunited."

Every year, on the Sabbath of "Behaalotecha," his friends organize a "Friends' Shabbat" in his memory. On the sixth anniversary of his death his friend Dudu Spiashvili wrote: "We, Mor's friends, sit and think of him every year: of his sweetness, his faith, his purity, of the amazing family he left behind, which was – and is now even more – the family of us all... Mor studied at the Dimona yeshiva, later known as the Mitzpe Ramon yeshiva, and began serving three years in Golani out of faith and a sense of mission of giving and love. Afterwards he thought and planned to continue his studies in yeshiva. He was one of the righteous of his generation, one of those who did everything simply, without boasting, but out of pride and a divine and national mission..."

Shalom Sapir (Spiashvili), who guided Mor in the movement and was a close friend, wrote him a letter: "On the Sabbath before you were killed, all of us guys were in Tira, a rare event. The Sabbath after Shavuot, exactly as this year. We sat in the garden between Minchah and Maariv, as if in your honor, to give a last honor. On Saturday night I last saw you. I knew you from elementary school as the eldest son of the teacher Yael Tziada. You were a small child with lots of curls and a smile that began and never ended. Later I became your madrich in Bnei Akiva. You were then in eighth grade. In activities you stubbornly refused to submit to the 'seriousness' I demanded of you. Maybe because deep inside you felt that everything was just talk, and you were a man of deeds. But back then I still did not understand that. Later you became a madrich yourself, and I remember watching from the side and seeing what a wonderful and charming person you were growing into. Very quickly the age gap between us became insignificant, and I found a wonderful friend, funny, wise, of rare character, and yet full of humility. A friend it was so good to be with, and whom we refused to part from. When you were in twelfth grade you told me you were going to study at a hesder yeshiva. Hesder students study in yeshiva for a year and a half, then enlist and serve a year and a half in combat units, and afterwards return to yeshiva for two more years of study. But that arrangement did not suit your plans. You knew you would serve three full years in the army. It was not that Torah study was not important to you – a righteous man like you – but you were simply not one to make concessions or shortcuts: if everyone serves three years, then so will you. Providence arranged it, and you enlisted in Golani, in the 51st Battalion. You were proud, not proud of your own small personal achievements, but proud of the privilege that had fallen to your lot. I say 'providence arranged it,' because I know you reached the right place, the place where people say little and do much, love the craft and hate pompous talk, in the deep sense of the word. True, you were not silent. You were full of stories, and you did complain. But even your complaints were charming. I always wanted to hear more about all those absurd moments in our army, which is the best army in the world, and about all the strange, funny and holy people who make it up. You completed the medic course and finally landed in the rifle company. We laughed about seniority and veteran status, about paratroopers and Golani. You were killed a few months before your discharge, shortly after you had earned your veteran status in the company..."

"I once wondered," a friend wrote on the sixth anniversary of his death, "what Mor's secret was. What was it about him that drew friends to him wherever he was? What was so calming and joyful about his presence? What was so charming about his smile? And I answered myself: modesty, simplicity. A presence that takes up no space. Happy is the one who tasted, felt and understood, who saw and learned while still alive. At some point it no longer surprised me how, whenever volunteers were needed, Mor volunteered. But it amazed me every time anew the way he volunteered. Mor taught those around him a lesson in how to volunteer, how to help. He volunteered quietly, without noise and without applause. He simply said, 'Yes, here I am.' I was not surprised to hear in the years after his death about the endless and blessed work at the Bnei Akiva branch in Tirat HaCarmel and beyond. But I was astonished: how was it that I never heard a word from him about it?"

Deputy Mayor and close family friend Arie Pargeon added that "Mor was a very idealistic person, who saw military service as a privilege and not a duty. He loved service very much and was proud to be a Golani fighter." A memorial monument was erected near the community center in his name, and the Bnei Akiva branch in Tirat HaCarmel, "Beit Mordehai," was named after him. At the Ephraim Tzemach elementary school in Tirat HaCarmel a beit midrash room was established in his name. At the "Midbara K'Eden" yeshiva in Mitzpe Ramon a Torah lecture hall was dedicated to his memory. His friends opened a group on Facebook called "Mordehai HaYehudi," intended for studying words of Torah in his memory and sharing memories.
